// The behavioural proof that the Bit COLLECTOR half of the safepoint handshake
// (#1633, runtime/stw/stw.bit) actually collects, that its root enumeration is
// complete, and that its sweep does not eat live objects.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ATTRIBUTION — WHY A COLLECTION COUNT HERE IS *THIS* COLLECTOR'S
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Pre-G2 (#1583) the deleted native runtime's `root` module was still linked
// and still owned the ABI name `bit_rt_safepoint`, so the poll codegen
// synthesized at every loop back edge went to that deleted runtime. Any
// `BIT_GC=stress BIT_GC_STATS=1` count read out of a normally linked binary
// was therefore that deleted runtime's collector's and was identical with or
// without this change — the trap #1632's agent measured (`collection 20000`)
// and correctly refused to claim.
//
// This test dodges it on both sides at once:
//
//   - The CODE under test is `stwPoll`, called by name. That is the Bit poll
//     body itself (`runtime/stw/stw.bit`), the function the `bit_rt_safepoint`
//     shim hands its snapshot to; nothing in the deleted native runtime's
//     `root` module can reach it.
//   - The HEAP under test is the `g` block below, a collector instance this
//     program builds over its own arena. The deleted native runtime's `root`
//     module's collector has never heard of it. Every number printed comes from `g`, so a non-zero
//     `gcStatCollections(g)` cannot have been produced by anything else.
//
// WHAT THIS CANNOT DRIVE, AND WHY (#1636). The natural end-to-end form would
// call the `@naked` shim `bit_rt_root_safepoint` so the snapshot is a real
// register/frame capture. It is not callable from Bit today: the INLINER inlines
// a `@naked` body into its caller, so the call site gets the shim's raw `ret`
// instead of a call and the caller returns with its frame never restored —
// filed as #1636 with a standalone repro. So the snapshot is supplied here
// instead, in exactly the `SafepointFrame` layout the shim would have built.
// The shim's own hand-encoded payload therefore remains UNEXECUTED by any test;
// that is #1636's to close, not this file's.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT EACH PHASE PROVES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   A — a poll COLLECTS, and every root class survives it. One payload is
//       reachable only as a buffered `is_ref` channel element (ABI.md §11), one
//       only as a registered task's `arg` (§5/§9), one only as a word on another
//       registered task's stack (§5). A fourth is reachable from nothing and
//       must be swept — without it the phase would pass on a collector that
//       marks everything.
//   B — remove all three roots, poll again, and all three are swept. This is
//       what proves the survivals in A were caused by the enumeration rather
//       than by a collection that never ran or never swept.
//   C — the PRECISE stack-map walk (`gcScanSnapshot`, §4) marks the slots a real
//       emitted stack map names. The table is decoded here independently of the
//       runtime's decoder, a frame is synthesized at a real
//       (code_addr + ret_off) safepoint, and the payload is planted in the first
//       slot that safepoint names. Marked -> survives; a second payload planted
//       nowhere is swept. Driven through the whole poll, not through
//       `gcScanSnapshot` alone, so the precise walk is proved to be WIRED INTO
//       the collection rather than merely to work in isolation.
//   D — the per-task SCRATCH area (#1679, #1683), the class that had no case
//       here and therefore no gate at all.
//   E — another stopped OS thread's PARKED FRAME (#1683), likewise.
//   F — the `bad=` counter the `BIT_GC_STATS` line grew in #1682, read through
//       the same bound-block accessor that line uses.
//   H — the RUNNING task's own stack (#1832). Unlike every phase above it does
//       NOT fabricate a stack: class 8 fires only for the task holding the real
//       `sp`, so the phase copies the real stack's bounds off the live current
//       task and lets the compiler place sixteen raw-`int` payloads, which
//       cannot all fit in the callee-saved file.
//   G — a parked task's own saved CALLEE-SAVED REGISTER FILE (#1742): the
//       `t[0..ctxWords)` block `schedSwitch` (`../../../runtime/sched`) writes
//       on every park. The task below has an EMPTY class-4 stack range (`sp`
//       set to its stack's own top) and a zero `arg`, so the only enumerated
//       class that can reach its payload is the ctx scan.
//   I — another stopped mutator's OWN STACK, conservatively (#1834). Phase E
//       proves that mutator's frame is walked PRECISELY; this proves the stack
//       BELOW that task's `ctxSp` and at or above the published frame is walked
//       CONSERVATIVELY, which is what a raw `int` needs and what a stack map
//       cannot give. Same synthetic-slot technique as E, with the payload
//       placed where every other class is empty by construction.
//   J — a thread parked in a `syscall` region (#1904, #2387): the class with
//       no case here and, until this change, no entry in `stw.bit`'s header
//       either — implemented and wired into `stwScanTasks` but enumerated
//       nowhere, so the declared/covered comparison below agreed with itself
//       while missing it entirely. Same `worldClaim` technique as E and I, but
//       published as `mSyscall` with a stack pointer (`stwSyscallLo`'s half)
//       rather than a frame, plus a second payload planted in the register
//       block `worldSyscallRegs` returns (`stwScanSyscallRegs`'s half, #1742's
//       gap over this state).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ONE CASE PER ROOT CLASS — THE RULE, AND WHY IT IS ENFORCED STATICALLY (#1683)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #1679 was a whole root class covered by NOTHING: the per-task scratch area
// held two live references (`scrElem`, a blocking channel send/receive's value
// slot, and `scrErr`, the pending-error object) and no enumerated class reached
// them. Everything stayed green — `./make`, `libbitrt`, `rootpins`,
// `rootabi`, `stwwiring`, `./make test`, `test-stress` and the full
// differential sweep — because a missing root class is not a missing symbol and
// not a wrong dump. It is a live object swept, which shows up later, somewhere
// else, as a SIGSEGV on one platform and a wrong answer with exit 0 on another.
//
// This file already had the shape of the answer and only covered the classes
// its author had thought of, which is precisely the failure mode: a class
// nobody wrote is a class nobody tests. So the rule is now mechanical rather
// than aspirational — `runtime/stw/stw.bit`'s ROOT SET header enumerates the
// classes, every one of them carries a `ROOT CLASS <n>` marker here, and
// the deleted harness `stwwiring`'s "every enumerated root class has a case" test fails
// if the two sets ever differ or if the count falls below the floor. Adding a
// class to the collector without a case here is a RED BUILD, not a silent hole.
//
// Every phase asserts a NON-ZERO collection count of its own (`ran=`), because
// a straight-line Bit program collects zero times under `BIT_GC=stress` — the
// backends emit safepoints only at loop back edges — and a survival check
// across a collection that never happened proves nothing at all.

import { spinUnlocked } from "../../../runtime"
import { heapInit, heapWords } from "../../../runtime/alloc"
import { gcInit, gcConfigure, gcAlloc, gcOwns, gcNumObjects, gcStatCollections } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { smFormatVersion } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { gcWords, gcBindMemory, storeWord, loadWord, loadByte, reserveWords } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { worldWords, worldClaim, worldRelease, maxMutators, mParked, muWords } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { mSyscall, worldSyscallRegs, worldSyscallRegWords } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { rtChanMake, chanBootBind, chanRegWords } from "../../../runtime/chan"
import { stwPollOn, stwAbandonedCount, stwBadOffsetsOn } from "../../../runtime/stw"
import { schedWords, scRegHead, taskWords, taskArg, taskState, taskParked } from "../../../runtime/sched"
import { schedCurrentTask, taskAt } from "../../../runtime/sched"
import { taskStackBase, taskStackBytes, ctxSp, ctxWords, taskRegNext } from "../../../runtime/sched"
import { chanScratchWords, scrElem, scrErr, scrIfaceOk } from "../../../runtime/sched"


fn main() {
  let arena = []i64(ARENA_WORDS)
  let indexArena = []i64(INDEX_WORDS)
  let heapBlock = []i64(heapWords)
  let heapLock = []i32(1)
  let state = []i64(gcWords)
  let meta = []i64(META_WORDS)

  // Blocks `boot` normally owns: the World/Mutator registry, a scheduler, the
  // channel reserve + registry, and one registered task.
  let worldBlock = []i64(worldWords)
  let schedBlock = []i64(schedWords)
  let chanReserve = []i64(CHAN_RESERVE_WORDS)
  let reserveDesc = []i64(reserveWords)
  let regBlock = []i64(chanRegWords)
  let fakeTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let fakeStack = []i64(TASK_STACK_WORDS)
  let longTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let longStack = []i64(LONG_STACK_WORDS)
  let frameBuf = []i64(FRAME_WORDS)
  let snapBuf = []i64(SNAP_WORDS)
  // Phase G's task and its (deliberately unused) stack — see phase G below.
  let ctxTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let ctxStack = []i64(TASK_STACK_WORDS)
  // Phase H's task. It has NO stack array of its own, and that is the whole
  // point — see phase H.
  let cls8Task = []i64(taskWords)
  // Phase I's task and the stack a synthesized parked mutator claims to be
  // standing in — see PARK_STACK_WORDS for why the geometry is what it is.
  let parkStackTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let parkedStack = []i64(PARK_STACK_WORDS)
  // Phase J's task and the stack a synthesized syscall-parked thread claims to
  // be standing in — see SYSCALL_STACK_WORDS for the geometry.
  let syscallTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let syscallStack = []i64(SYSCALL_STACK_WORDS)

  // Phase D's task. Its stack is exactly `chanScratchWords` words, so the whole
  // scratch area is mapped and the scan's `min(chanScratchWords, stackBytes/8)`
  // bound resolves to the full area — that is what lets the payload at the
  // LAST scratch word (`scrIfaceOk`, 266 — #3280 moved this off
  // `scrFsReadFailed`, 265, once `chanScratchWords` widened past it) be a floor
  // on the scanned set rather than a second copy of the first word's test.
  // `fakeStack` above stays 64 words on purpose: it is the block whose short
  // mapping caught the unbounded 265-word read (#1679, back when 265 was the
  // whole area).
  let scratchTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let scratchStack = []i64(chanScratchWords)
  // Phase E's parked-thread frame and the `SafepointFrame` that publishes it,
  // both kept apart from phase C's so that phase C's snapshot is not what phase
  // E ends up proving.
  let parkFrame = []i64(FRAME_WORDS)
  let parkSnap = []i64(SNAP_WORDS)

  let metaAddr = int(ptrOf(meta))
  meta[MI_NODE + 0] = 16
  meta[MI_NODE + 1] = 0
  meta[MI_NODE + 2] = 0
  meta[MI_BAD + 0] = 16
  meta[MI_BAD + 1] = metaAddr + MI_BAD_OFFS * 8
  meta[MI_BAD + 2] = 1
  meta[MI_BAD_OFFS] = 4
  let nodeInfo = metaAddr + MI_NODE * 8
  let refArrInfo = metaAddr + MI_REFARR * 8
  let badInfo = metaAddr + MI_BAD * 8

  let hp = ptrOf(heapBlock)
  heapInit(hp)
  let hlk = ptrOf(heapLock)
  atomicStore(hlk, spinUnlocked)

  let g = ptrOf(state)
  gcBindMemory(g, int(ptrOf(arena)), ARENA_WORDS * 8, int(ptrOf(indexArena)), INDEX_WORDS * 8, 4096)
  if (!gcInit(g, int(hp), int(hlk), int(hlk), refArrInfo, MARK_STACK)) {
    print("gcInit failed\n")
    return
  }
  // stress: every poll collects, ignoring the byte trigger — the precise-rooting
  // oracle. A missed root is swept on the very next poll.
  gcConfigure(g, true, true, MIN_TRIGGER, GROWTH_PCT, false)

  // Wire the registry the mutator half uses and the three blocks the collector
  // half enumerates roots from. `stwBind` is all-or-nothing by design: with any
  // one of them 0 the poll refuses to collect rather than collecting with a
  // smaller root set.
  chanBootBind(int(ptrOf(reserveDesc)), int(ptrOf(chanReserve)), CHAN_RESERVE_WORDS * 8,
               int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)))

  // ROOT CLASS 1: a buffered `is_ref` channel, built by the shipped constructor
  // so the registry entry is the real one.
  let handle = rtChanMake(CAP, true)
  if (handle == 0) {
    print("chan_make returned nil\n")
    return
  }
  let pChan = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pChan, M_CHAN)
  storeWord(loadWord(handle + CH_BUF * 8), pChan)
  storeWord(handle + CH_COUNT * 8, 1)

  // ROOT CLASS 4 and ROOT CLASS 5: one registered task, parked, whose stack
  // holds one payload (class 4) and whose `arg` holds another (class 5) —
  // `stack=` and `arg=` in phase A are one assertion each. The scheduler block
  // is zeroed, so its registry lock is already `spinUnlocked` and its head is 0
  // until set here.
  let pArg = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pArg, M_ARG)
  let pStack = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pStack, M_STACK)

  // The class-5 task, on the SHORT stack. Its own stack holds nothing: `arg` is
  // the only root it contributes, so `arg=` cannot be carried by the stack walk.
  let t = ptrOf(fakeTask)
  *(t + taskArg) = pArg
  *(t + taskState) = taskParked
  *(t + taskStackBase) = int(ptrOf(fakeStack))
  *(t + taskStackBytes) = TASK_STACK_WORDS * 8
  *(t + ctxSp) = int(ptrOf(fakeStack))

  // The class-4 task, on the long stack, with `sp` one word past the scratch
  // area and `arg` empty — see LONG_STACK_WORDS for why that placement is the
  // whole point.
  let lt = ptrOf(longTask)
  longStack[LONG_STACK_SLOT] = pStack
  *(lt + taskArg) = 0
  *(lt + taskState) = taskParked
  *(lt + taskStackBase) = int(ptrOf(longStack))
  *(lt + taskStackBytes) = LONG_STACK_WORDS * 8
  *(lt + ctxSp) = int(ptrOf(longStack)) + chanScratchWords * 8

  // Both on the all-tasks registry, in the intrusive `regNext` list the
  // collector walks.
  *(t + taskRegNext) = int(lt)
  *(lt + taskRegNext) = 0
  *(ptrOf(schedBlock) + scRegHead) = int(t)

  // Reachable from nothing. Without this the phase would pass on a collector
  // that simply marks every object it has.
  let pDead = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pDead, M_DEAD)

  // ---- phase A: poll, and read the count out of OUR collector -------------
  // A snapshot whose `ret` is 0 is exactly what the shim publishes from a door
  // that holds no stack map, and it makes the precise walk a no-op here — phase
  // C is where that walk is put under load.
  let snap = int(ptrOf(snapBuf))
  storeWord(snap + 0, 0)
  storeWord(snap + 8, 0)
  stwPollOn(int(g), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(worldBlock)), snap)
  print("A ran=${gcStatCollections(g) > 0} chan=${alive(g, pChan, M_CHAN)} arg=${alive(g, pArg, M_ARG)} stack=${alive(g, pStack, M_STACK)} dead=${gcOwns(g, pDead)} abandoned=${stwAbandonedCount()}\n")

  // ---- phase B: drop every root, poll again, everything goes --------------
  storeWord(handle + CH_COUNT * 8, 0)
  *(t + taskArg) = 0
  longStack[LONG_STACK_SLOT] = 0
  let cB = gcStatCollections(g)
  stwPollOn(int(g), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(worldBlock)), snap)
  print("B ran=${gcStatCollections(g) > cB} chan=${gcOwns(g, pChan)} arg=${gcOwns(g, pArg)} stack=${gcOwns(g, pStack)} live=${gcNumObjects(g)}\n")

  // ---- phase C: ROOT CLASS 2, the precise stack-map walk over THIS thread's
  // own published snapshot: it marks what a real emitted stack map names ------
  let base = mapsBegin()
  let tblLen = mapsEnd() - base
  let hitPc = 0
  let hitSlot = 0
  let off = 0
  // Set true if any entry's stamped version disagrees with this tree's own
  // (#3189): this decoder is deliberately independent of
  // runtime/gc/stackmap.bit's reader (see this file's header), so it must
  // track the same fixed-header field that reader checks, or a wire-format
  // desync here reports LESS rather than refusing — exactly the failure mode
  // #3189 exists to make loud. This is the THIRD hand decoder of this format
  // (compiler's writer, the runtime reader, this one); #1927 updated the
  // first two and missed this one.
  let vBad = false
  // Bounded by the extent; every entry is self-delimiting.
  while (off < tblLen) {
    let codeAddr = u64At(base + off)
    off = off + 8
    let version = u16At(base + off)
    off = off + 2
    if (version != smFormatVersion) {
      vBad = true
    }
    let codeSize = u32At(base + off)
    off = off + 4
    let numSaved = u16At(base + off)
    off = off + 2 + numSaved * 6
    let numSps = u16At(base + off)
    off = off + 2
    let si = 0
    // Bounded: this entry's safepoint count.
    while (si < numSps) {
      let retOff = u32At(base + off)
      off = off + 4
      let numSlots = u16At(base + off)
      off = off + 2
      let slotsOff = off
      off = off + numSlots * 4
      let numRegs = u16At(base + off)
      off = off + 2 + numRegs * 2
      if (hitPc == 0 && numSlots > 0 && usableSlot(i32At(base + slotsOff))) {
        hitPc = codeAddr + retOff
        hitSlot = i32At(base + slotsOff)
      }
      si = si + 1
    }

    // Round the cursor up past this entry's trailing pad (#1927, ABI.md §4):
    // the writer pads every entry to a multiple of 8 bytes so every atom is
    // 8-aligned, and the walk must round up by the same rule or it lands
    // inside this entry's padding instead of at the next entry's start. Same
    // rule as runtime/gc/stackmap.bit's reader.
    let rem = off % 8
    if (rem != 0) {
      off = off + 8 - rem
    }
  }

  let pPrecise = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pPrecise, M_PRECISE)
  let pMissed = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pMissed, M_MISSED)

  let fp = int(ptrOf(frameBuf)) + FRAME_MID * 8
  storeWord(fp, 0)                 // saved fp of 0 stops the walk after one frame
  storeWord(fp + 8, 0)             // and its return address is never followed
  storeWord(fp + hitSlot, pPrecise)
  storeWord(snap + 0, hitPc)       // ret: the safepoint this stack map describes
  storeWord(snap + 8, fp)          // fp: the synthetic frame above
  let cC = gcStatCollections(g)
  stwPollOn(int(g), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(worldBlock)), snap)
  print("C ran=${gcStatCollections(g) > cC} found=${hitPc != 0} precise=${alive(g, pPrecise, M_PRECISE)} missed=${gcOwns(g, pMissed)} vOK=${!vBad}\n")

  // ---- phase D: ROOT CLASS 6, the per-task SCRATCH area (#1679) ------------
  //
  // THE CLASS THAT HAD NO CASE, and the reason this file grew (#1683). The
  // scratch is the `chanScratchWords` words at the BASE of a task's stack, and
  // a stack grows DOWN from its top, so the whole area sits strictly BELOW
  // `ctx.sp` — outside class 4's `[sp, top)` range for every task, always, by
  // construction. No stack map names those words either, so classes 2 and 3
  // cannot see them and the RUNNING task is as exposed as a parked one.
  //
  // The task below makes that separation explicit rather than incidental: its
  // `arg` is 0 (class 5 covers nothing) and its `sp` is set to the TOP of its
  // stack, so the class-4 range is EMPTY. The only enumerated class that can
  // reach these three payloads is the scratch scan.
  //
  // Three payloads, not one, and the third is a floor on the SCANNED SET
  // rather than a repeat: `scrElem` (word 5) is the blocking channel
  // send/receive slot and `scrErr` (word 7) is the pending-error object — the
  // two references #1679 actually lost — while `scrIfaceOk` (word 266) is
  // the LAST word of the area. A scan that walks a prefix, or that enumerates
  // only the two named words instead of the whole area, keeps the first two
  // alive and drops the third. #3280 moved this payload here from
  // `scrFsReadFailed` (word 265) when `chanScratchWords` widened to 267:
  // leaving it at 265 would have kept passing while proving strictly less — a
  // 266-of-267-word scan would still find it, so the phase would no longer
  // prove the full area is scanned, which is the entire point of the third
  // payload.
  let pElem = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pElem, M_SCR_ELEM)
  let pErr = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pErr, M_SCR_ERR)
  let pLast = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pLast, M_SCR_LAST)
  let pScrDead = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pScrDead, M_SCR_DEAD)

  scratchStack[scrElem] = pElem
  scratchStack[scrErr] = pErr
  scratchStack[scrIfaceOk] = pLast

  let t2 = ptrOf(scratchTask)
  *(t2 + taskArg) = 0
  *(t2 + taskState) = taskParked
  *(t2 + taskStackBase) = int(ptrOf(scratchStack))
  *(t2 + taskStackBytes) = chanScratchWords * 8
  *(t2 + ctxSp) = int(ptrOf(scratchStack)) + chanScratchWords * 8
  *(ptrOf(schedBlock) + scRegHead) = int(t2)

  // Back to a snapshot that names nothing, so class 2 cannot carry this phase.
  storeWord(snap + 0, 0)
  storeWord(snap + 8, 0)
  let cD = gcStatCollections(g)
  stwPollOn(int(g), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(worldBlock)), snap)
  print("D ran=${gcStatCollections(g) > cD} elem=${alive(g, pElem, M_SCR_ELEM)} err=${alive(g, pErr, M_SCR_ERR)} last=${alive(g, pLast, M_SCR_LAST)} dead=${gcOwns(g, pScrDead)} abandoned=${stwAbandonedCount()}\n")

  // ---- phase E: ROOT CLASS 3, another stopped OS thread's PARKED FRAME -----
  //
  // The collector walks every OTHER registered mutator's published
  // `SafepointFrame` precisely. Driving that with a real second OS thread would
  // need one parked at a safepoint for the whole collection; the registry is
  // plain memory under a stopped world, so a slot is synthesized instead —
  // claimed through the shipped `worldClaim`, then published as parked with a
  // frame, in the same three words a real parking thread writes.
  //
  // `acked` must carry the epoch of the collection that is ABOUT to run, or
  // `worldStoppedFor` refuses to count the slot as stopped and the rendezvous
  // expires instead of collecting. That is why the epoch word is read first and
  // incremented by one: `worldRendezvous` bumps it exactly once per collection.
  // A mis-set slot is loud rather than silent — `ran=false abandoned=1`.
  let pPark = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pPark, M_PARKED)
  let pParkDead = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pParkDead, M_PARKED_DEAD)

  let fp2 = int(ptrOf(parkFrame)) + FRAME_MID * 8
  storeWord(fp2, 0)
  storeWord(fp2 + 8, 0)
  storeWord(fp2 + hitSlot, pPark)

  // What a parking thread publishes is its `SafepointFrame` (ABI.md §4), NOT
  // its frame pointer — `worldPark(world, slot, frame)` is handed the same
  // `snap` the shim built. Publishing the frame itself instead reads its first
  // word as `ret`, finds 0 and walks nothing, which passes every structural
  // check and marks nothing: measured here as `parked=false` before the fix.
  let parkSnapAddr = int(ptrOf(parkSnap))
  storeWord(parkSnapAddr + 0, hitPc)
  storeWord(parkSnapAddr + 8, fp2)

  let wb = ptrOf(worldBlock)
  let slot = worldClaim(wb)
  let epoch = worldBlock[maxMutators * muWords + MU_EPOCH_IDX] + 1
  worldBlock[slot * muWords + MU_FRAME] = parkSnapAddr
  worldBlock[slot * muWords + MU_ACKED] = epoch
  worldBlock[slot * muWords + MU_STATE] = mParked

  let cE = gcStatCollections(g)
  stwPollOn(int(g), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(worldBlock)), snap)
  print("E ran=${gcStatCollections(g) > cE} other=${slot > 0} parked=${alive(g, pPark, M_PARKED)} missed=${gcOwns(g, pParkDead)} abandoned=${stwAbandonedCount()}\n")
  // RELEASED IMMEDIATELY, and it must be: the slot stays `parked` with a now
  // stale `acked`, so leaving it registered would make every later rendezvous
  // spin to its bound and abandon.
  worldRelease(wb, slot)

  // ---- phase F: the `bad=` counter is real and reachable (#1682) -----------
  //
  // `gcStatBadOffsets` counts pointer-map offsets the tracer REFUSED —
  // misaligned, or running past the body — and skips the field instead of
  // reading out of bounds. Until #1682 no linked binary could report it, so a
  // degraded trace (fields skipped, referents unmarked, live objects swept)
  // read exactly like a healthy one. The `BIT_GC_STATS` line now carries it,
  // through `stwBadOffsets` — and `stwBadOffsetsOn` read here is that same
  // accessor over THIS program's collector rather than the process-global one,
  // so it is still the field's value and not a parallel path. Reading the
  // global form here silently reported the LIVE runtime's counter instead
  // (#1836): `bad=` went false while the malformed TypeInfo was traced
  // correctly in this program's own collector.
  //
  // An always-zero field would be worse than none, which is what the two halves
  // below assert: 0 across five clean collections, and non-zero the moment one
  // malformed `TypeInfo` is traced.
  let bad0 = stwBadOffsetsOn(int(g))
  let pBad = gcAlloc(g, badInfo)
  storeWord(pBad, M_BADMAP)
  scratchStack[scrElem] = pBad
  let cF = gcStatCollections(g)
  stwPollOn(int(g), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(worldBlock)), snap)
  print("F ran=${gcStatCollections(g) > cF} clean=${bad0} bad=${stwBadOffsetsOn(int(g)) > 0} alive=${alive(g, pBad, M_BADMAP)}\n")

  // ---- phase G: ROOT CLASS 7, a parked task's own saved register file (#1742)
  //
  // `stwScanStackRange` (class 4) covers `[ctxSp, stackTop)` — the task's
  // STACK. `schedSwitch` (`../../../runtime/sched`) does not spill the
  // callee-saved file there: it writes x19..x28 (arm64) / rbx,r12..r15 (x64)
  // into the TASK'S OWN CONTROL BLOCK, `t[0..ctxWords)`, a separate
  // allocation. A value the register allocator keeps live in one of those
  // registers across a park is therefore outside class 4's range by
  // construction and needs its own scan (`stwScanCtx`).
  //
  // The task below makes the separation explicit exactly as phase D's does
  // for the scratch area: `arg` is 0 (class 5 covers nothing) and `sp` is set
  // to the TOP of its own stack, so the class-4 range is EMPTY. `ctxStack` is
  // mapped only so `taskStackBase`/`taskStackBytes` are non-degenerate — the
  // payload never touches it, so class 6's scratch scan (which reads the
  // stack's OWN base, not the control block) cannot carry this phase either.
  // The only enumerated class that can reach `pCtx` is the ctx scan.
  let pCtx = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pCtx, M_CTX)
  let pCtxDead = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pCtxDead, M_CTX_DEAD)

  let ct = ptrOf(ctxTask)
  *(ct + taskArg) = 0
  *(ct + taskState) = taskParked
  *(ct + taskStackBase) = int(ptrOf(ctxStack))
  *(ct + taskStackBytes) = TASK_STACK_WORDS * 8
  *(ct + ctxSp) = int(ptrOf(ctxStack)) + TASK_STACK_WORDS * 8
  *(ct + taskRegNext) = 0
  *(ptrOf(schedBlock) + scRegHead) = int(ct)
  ctxTask[CTX_SLOT] = pCtx

  storeWord(snap + 0, 0)
  storeWord(snap + 8, 0)
  let cG = gcStatCollections(g)
  stwPollOn(int(g), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(worldBlock)), snap)
  print("G ran=${gcStatCollections(g) > cG} ctx=${alive(g, pCtx, M_CTX)} dead=${gcOwns(g, pCtxDead)} abandoned=${stwAbandonedCount()}\n")

  // ---- phase H: ROOT CLASS 8, the RUNNING task's own stack (#1832) ---------
  //
  // Every phase above FABRICATES its task: a module-level `[]i64` stands in for
  // a stack and the phase writes its payload into a known slot of it. THAT
  // CANNOT WORK HERE. Class 8 fires only for the task holding the REAL stack
  // pointer (`stwTaskHoldsSp`), and a fabricated stack never does — module data
  // sits far below the stack in the address space, so no `[stackBase, +bytes)`
  // range can contain both a module array and `sp`.
  //
  // Bit also has no stack-allocated aggregate to plant into: `[]i64(n)` is a
  // heap slice and a module fixed array is `__DATA`, so there is no way to place
  // a word at a chosen address on the running stack.
  //
  // So this phase does the opposite of fabricate. It COPIES the real stack's
  // bounds off the live current task, registers them on its own task block, and
  // lets the COMPILER place the payloads — which is exactly the shape of the
  // defect: `bit_rt_chan_send` holds the element it is about to store as a bare
  // `int`, because E0079 forbids a pinned symbol naming a managed type, and a
  // stack map names only reference-typed slots.
  //
  // Why sixteen payloads: a raw `int` held across the `stwPoll` call must live
  // either in a callee-saved register or in a stack slot. Sixteen simultaneously
  // live values cannot all be in the callee-saved file (ten on arm64, five on
  // x64), so any allocator spills at least six to the stack, where class 8 is
  // the only enumerated class that reaches them. A single payload could have
  // spent its whole life in a register and proven nothing.
  //
  // Every other class is empty for this task BY CONSTRUCTION, so the assertion
  // cannot be carried by one of them: `arg` is 0 (class 5); `ctxSp` is the TOP
  // of the stack, so class 4's `[ctxSp, top)` range is empty; `running` is true,
  // so class 7's ctx scan is skipped; class 6's scratch scan reads the real
  // stack's lowest 266 words (`chanScratchWords`), thousands of words BELOW
  // `sp` and below anything a live frame occupies; and `snap` carries `ret = 0`
  // so class 2 returns before walking. Deleting class 8's `lo = sp` reverts the
  // range to `[ctxSp, top)` — empty — and must redden THIS phase alone.
  //
  // `schedCurrentTask()` is 0 when no green task owns this stack, which is the
  // case against the deleted native runtime's `libbitrt.a`. Reported rather than skipped
  // silently, so the phase can never read as green while asserting nothing.
  let curTask = schedCurrentTask()
  let onTask = curTask != 0
  let cls8Alive = 0
  let cls8Dead = 0
  if (onTask) {
    let real = taskAt(curTask)
    let ht = ptrOf(cls8Task)
    *(ht + taskArg) = 0
    *(ht + taskState) = taskParked
    *(ht + taskStackBase) = *(real + taskStackBase)
    *(ht + taskStackBytes) = *(real + taskStackBytes)
    *(ht + ctxSp) = *(real + taskStackBase) + *(real + taskStackBytes)
    *(ht + taskRegNext) = 0
    *(ptrOf(schedBlock) + scRegHead) = int(ht)

    // A control object allocated and then dropped, so this phase also shows the
    // collector still SWEEPS — a class-8 scan that marked the whole stack
    // indiscriminately would keep this too and the phase would pass for the
    // wrong reason.
    let pGone = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    storeWord(pGone, M_CLS8 + 1)
    let gone = pGone

    let p0 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p1 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p2 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p3 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p4 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p5 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p6 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p7 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p8 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p9 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p10 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p11 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p12 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p13 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p14 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    let p15 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    storeWord(p0, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p1, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p2, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p3, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p4, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p5, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p6, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p7, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p8, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p9, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p10, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p11, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p12, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p13, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p14, M_CLS8)
    storeWord(p15, M_CLS8)

    storeWord(snap + 0, 0)
    storeWord(snap + 8, 0)
    let cH = gcStatCollections(g)
    stwPollOn(int(g), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(worldBlock)), snap)
    let ranH = gcStatCollections(g) > cH

    // Counted, not `&&`-ed: the count is what distinguishes "class 8 reaches the
    // spilled slots" from "the allocator happened to keep them all in registers".
    if (alive(g, p0, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p1, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p2, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p3, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p4, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p5, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p6, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p7, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p8, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p9, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p10, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p11, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p12, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p13, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p14, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (alive(g, p15, M_CLS8)) { cls8Alive = cls8Alive + 1 }
    if (gcOwns(g, gone)) { cls8Dead = 1 }
    print("H ran=${ranH} onTask=true alive=${cls8Alive}/${CLS8_COUNT} abandoned=${stwAbandonedCount()}\n")
  }
  if (!onTask) {
    print("H ran=false onTask=false alive=0/${CLS8_COUNT} abandoned=${stwAbandonedCount()}\n")
  }

  // ---- phase I: ROOT CLASS 10, another stopped mutator's OWN STACK (#1834) --
  //
  // Class 8 lowers the conservative bound for the stack holding the LIVE `sp`.
  // There is exactly one live `sp` and it belongs to the collector, so every
  // OTHER thread stopped at a safepoint keeps class 4's `ctxSp` bound — where
  // its task last PARKED, above the frames it is actually sitting in. What is
  // left covering it is class 3's PRECISE walk, and a stack map names only
  // reference-typed slots, so a GC object held as a bare `int` (which the ABI
  // boundary FORCES on every pinned symbol, E0079) is invisible to it. Two
  // threads inside `bit_rt_chan_send` at once is one scanned stack and one
  // unscanned one.
  //
  // Driving that with a real second OS thread would need one parked at a
  // safepoint for the whole collection, so a slot is synthesized exactly as
  // phase E does — claimed through the shipped `worldClaim`, published as
  // `parked` with a frame, acking the epoch the next collection will use.
  //
  // WHAT IS DIFFERENT FROM PHASE E is which half of that slot is under test.
  // Phase E plants its payload INSIDE the published frame, where the precise
  // walk finds it. This one plants the payload on the STACK, at a word no stack
  // map names and no other class reaches, and publishes the frame ADDRESS as
  // the only evidence of where that thread is. The frame's own first word is 0,
  // so `gcScanSnapshot` walks nothing and cannot carry this phase.
  //
  // Registering `parkStackTask` alone also unregisters phase H's task, which is
  // required: while that one is registered its mapping spans the REAL stack, so
  // class 8 would conservatively scan main's own frames and keep `pStack10`
  // alive no matter what class 10 did.
  let pStack10 = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pStack10, M_CLS10)
  let pStack10Dead = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pStack10Dead, M_CLS10_DEAD)

  let parkBase = int(ptrOf(parkedStack))
  parkedStack[PARK_FRAME_SLOT] = 0            // ret == 0: class 3 walks nothing
  parkedStack[PARK_FRAME_SLOT + 1] = 0        // fp, never followed
  parkedStack[PARK_PAYLOAD_SLOT] = pStack10

  let pt = ptrOf(parkStackTask)
  *(pt + taskArg) = 0
  *(pt + taskState) = taskParked
  *(pt + taskStackBase) = parkBase
  *(pt + taskStackBytes) = PARK_STACK_WORDS * 8
  *(pt + ctxSp) = parkBase + PARK_STACK_WORDS * 8
  *(pt + taskRegNext) = 0
  *(ptrOf(schedBlock) + scRegHead) = int(pt)

  let slot10 = worldClaim(wb)
  let epoch10 = worldBlock[maxMutators * muWords + MU_EPOCH_IDX] + 1
  worldBlock[slot10 * muWords + MU_FRAME] = parkBase + PARK_FRAME_SLOT * 8
  worldBlock[slot10 * muWords + MU_ACKED] = epoch10
  worldBlock[slot10 * muWords + MU_STATE] = mParked

  storeWord(snap + 0, 0)
  storeWord(snap + 8, 0)
  let cI = gcStatCollections(g)
  stwPollOn(int(g), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(worldBlock)), snap)
  print("I ran=${gcStatCollections(g) > cI} other=${slot10 > 0} stack=${alive(g, pStack10, M_CLS10)} missed=${gcOwns(g, pStack10Dead)} abandoned=${stwAbandonedCount()}\n")
  // Released for the reason phase E's is: the slot stays `parked` with a now
  // stale `acked`, so leaving it registered would make every later rendezvous
  // spin to its bound and abandon.
  worldRelease(wb, slot10)

  // ---- phase J: ROOT CLASS 9, a thread parked in a `syscall` region -------
  //
  // #1904's state for a thread blocked in the kernel while still holding live
  // Bit references — a subprocess `waitpid`, `runtime/ABI.md` §5's "syscall"
  // contract. Neither `blocked` (no live references, by contract) nor
  // `running` (waited for) fits it, so the collector scans it where it stands
  // instead: the lowest published `sp` (`stwSyscallLo`) plus the saved
  // callee-saved register file (`stwScanSyscallRegs`, #1742's gap over this
  // state). This is #2387 exactly: the class was implemented and wired into
  // `stwScanTasks` without ever being added to `stw.bit`'s ROOT SET header, so
  // the declared/covered comparison above agreed with itself while missing it
  // — no case here could have existed for a class the header did not name.
  //
  // Same `worldClaim` technique as phases E and I, but published as
  // `mSyscall` rather than `mParked`. That needs no epoch/`acked` bookkeeping
  // at all: `worldStoppedFor` counts a `syscall` slot as stopped
  // unconditionally (#1904), unlike a `parked` one.
  //
  // Geometry mirrors phase I's: `ctxSp` at the stack's own TOP empties class
  // 4's own contribution, the published `sp` sits at word 300 — above the
  // 266-word scratch area, so class 6 cannot reach it either — and the stack
  // payload at word 400 sits strictly between that `sp` and `ctxSp`, so only
  // class 9's lowering of the scan's low bound brings it into the scanned
  // range. The register payload is reachable ONLY through
  // `stwScanSyscallRegs`, which class 9's slot lookup runs whenever the
  // published `sp` belongs to this task, regardless of where the resulting
  // low bound ends up.
  let pSysStack = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pSysStack, M_SYSCALL)
  let pSysReg = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pSysReg, M_SYSCALL_REG)
  let pSysDead = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(pSysDead, M_SYSCALL_DEAD)

  let syscallBase = int(ptrOf(syscallStack))
  syscallStack[SYSCALL_PAYLOAD_SLOT] = pSysStack

  let st = ptrOf(syscallTask)
  *(st + taskArg) = 0
  *(st + taskState) = taskParked
  *(st + taskStackBase) = syscallBase
  *(st + taskStackBytes) = SYSCALL_STACK_WORDS * 8
  *(st + ctxSp) = syscallBase + SYSCALL_STACK_WORDS * 8
  *(st + taskRegNext) = 0
  *(ptrOf(schedBlock) + scRegHead) = int(st)

  let slotJ = worldClaim(wb)
  worldBlock[slotJ * muWords + MU_SP] = syscallBase + SYSCALL_SP_SLOT * 8
  worldBlock[slotJ * muWords + MU_STATE] = mSyscall
  storeWord(worldSyscallRegs(slotJ), pSysReg)

  storeWord(snap + 0, 0)
  storeWord(snap + 8, 0)
  let cJ = gcStatCollections(g)
  stwPollOn(int(g), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(worldBlock)), snap)
  print("J ran=${gcStatCollections(g) > cJ} other=${slotJ > 0} stack=${alive(g, pSysStack, M_SYSCALL)} regs=${alive(g, pSysReg, M_SYSCALL_REG)} missed=${gcOwns(g, pSysDead)} abandoned=${stwAbandonedCount()}\n")
  // Released for the reason phases E and I's are: leaving the slot `syscall`
  // would make every later rendezvous conservatively scan a stale sp and
  // register block that no longer describes a live thread.
  worldRelease(wb, slotJ)

  // Hold every backing slice to the end of `main`, so only raw untraced pointers
  // ever reach the collector under test and the LIVE (libbitrt) collector cannot
  // move the ground under it when the suite reruns under BIT_GC=stress.
  print("held ${len(arena) + len(indexArena) + len(heapBlock) + len(heapLock) + len(state) + len(meta)}\n")
  print("held ${len(worldBlock) + len(schedBlock) + len(chanReserve) + len(reserveDesc) + len(regBlock) + len(fakeTask) + len(fakeStack) + len(longTask) + len(longStack) + len(frameBuf) + len(snapBuf)}\n")
  print("held ${len(scratchTask) + len(scratchStack) + len(parkFrame) + len(parkSnap)}\n")
  print("held ${len(ctxTask) + len(ctxStack) + len(cls8Task) + len(parkStackTask) + len(parkedStack)}\n")
  print("held ${len(syscallTask) + len(syscallStack)}\n")
}
